    "q-326002": {
      "dom_id": "step-q326002",
      "name": "Question 3",
      "problem": "Solve the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\ngiven that the general solution to our differential equation is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nThe solution to the initial value problem is\n\n| $x(t)=\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-1}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{t}+\\,\\,$ |  | {{field-2}} |  | $\\,\\,{e}^{-t}.$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n|  | {{field-3}} |  |  | {{field-4}} |  |  |  |  |",
      "worked_solution": "To find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ in the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2} \\\\ 2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}=5 \\\\ 2{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the first equation by $-2$ and adding the result to the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n-7{c}_{2}=-7\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=1.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+3\\cdot 1=5\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=2.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =2\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+1\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}+[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
