      "source_example_id": "e-5609",
      "knowledge_point": "Solving an Initial Value Problem Given the Eigenvectors and Eigenvalues of the Corresponding Matrix",
      "kp_id": "e-5609"
    },
    "q-39280": {
      "dom_id": "step-q39280",
      "name": "Question 8",
      "problem": "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $x(t),$ and that $x(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nfind the position of the particle at $t=7$ seconds.",
      "worked_solution": "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=0$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2} \\\\ -{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}+4{c}_{2}=6 \\\\ -{c}_{1}+3{c}_{2}=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMultiplying the second equation by $-1$ and adding the result to the first equation, we get\n\n$$\n{c}_{2}=6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the first equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n-{c}_{1}+4\\cdot 6=6\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=18.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =18\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+6\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $x(7)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(7) & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4\\cdot 7}+[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-7} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18 \\\\ -18\\end{aligned}]{e}^{28}+[\\begin{aligned}24 \\\\ 18\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-7} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-18{e}^{28}+24{e}^{-7} \\\\ -18{e}^{28}+18{e}^{-7}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
