    "q-79729": {
      "dom_id": "step-q79729",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Let $A$ be a $2\\times 2$ matrix with eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-2$ corresponding to the eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ respectively, where\n\n$$\n{v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],\\,{v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nGiven that the position of a particle at time $t$ seconds is given by the vector $x(t),$ and that $x(t)$ is the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\nfind the position of the particle at $t=3$ seconds.",
      "worked_solution": "Given a system of first-order linear ODEs in the form\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ has two distinct eigenvalues ${\\lambda }_{1},{\\lambda }_{2}$ with corresponding eigenvectors ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2},$ the formula for the general solution is\n\n$$\nx(t)={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the general solution to our matrix differential equation is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, to find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}$, we substitute $t=3$ into the general solution:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{0} & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2} \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 4\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{c}_{1}-2{c}_{2}=2 \\\\ {c}_{1}+{c}_{2}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get\n\n$$\n-{c}_{2}=6\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=-6.\n$$\n\nSubstituting this into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n{c}_{1}+(-6)=4\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}=10.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =10\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+(-6)\\cdot [\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3t}+[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate $x(3)$:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(3) & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]{e}^{3\\cdot 3}+[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-2\\cdot 3} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]{e}^{9}+[\\begin{aligned}12 \\\\ -6\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-6} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-10{e}^{9}+12{e}^{-6} \\\\ 10{e}^{9}-6{e}^{-6}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
