    "q-327159": {
      "dom_id": "step-q327159",
      "name": "Question 13",
      "problem": "Find the solution to the initial value problem\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=2{x}_{1}(t)-3{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{1}(t)+4{x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned},\\,x(1)=[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\n*Hint: The eigenvalues of the system's matrix are ${\\lambda }_{1}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=1.$*",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- We are given that the eigenvalues are\n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=5\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=5,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & -3 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -3 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=1$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(1) & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]e & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{5}{c}_{1}+3e{c}_{2} \\\\ {e}^{5}{c}_{1}+e{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}5{e}^{2} \\\\ 3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}-{e}^{5}{c}_{1}+3e{c}_{2}=5{e}^{2} \\\\ {e}^{5}{c}_{1}+e{c}_{2}=3{e}^{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the equations, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4e{c}_{2}=8{e}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{2}=2e.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${c}_{2}=2e$ into the second equation, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{5}{c}_{1}+e(2e)=3{e}^{2}\\,⟹\\,{c}_{1}={e}^{-3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{-3}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{5t}+2e[\\begin{aligned}3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]{e}^{t}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
