    "q-324462": {
      "dom_id": "step-q324462",
      "name": "Question 14",
      "problem": "Find the solution to the following initial value problem.\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=4{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we write down the matrix differential equation ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)$ for the given system:\n\n$$\n[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nWe need to find the eigenvalues and the eigenvectors of the matrix $A.$\n\n- Since $A$ is upper-triangular, the eigenvalues are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=4\\,\\text{and}\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=-1.\n$$\n\n- Let's go through the process of finding the eigenvectors.\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{1}=4,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{1}I=[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{aligned}]∼[\\begin{aligned}0 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{1}=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].$\n\n- For ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1,$ we get \n\n$$\nA-{\\lambda }_{2}I=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nSo, we get an eigenvector \n${v}_{2}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}].$\n\nNow, we write down the general solution to the given linear system of differential equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={c}_{1}{v}_{1}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{1}t}+{c}_{2}{v}_{2}{e}^{{\\lambda }_{2}t} \\\\  & ={c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t},\\,{c}_{1},{c}_{2}\\in R\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we find the values of ${c}_{1}$ and ${c}_{2}.$ Substituting $t=0$ into the general solution, we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(0) & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4(0)}+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-0} & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ {c}_{1}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]+{c}_{2}[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}] \\\\ [\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-{c}_{2} \\\\ 5{c}_{2}\\end{aligned}] & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 10\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have a system of linear equations\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}{c}_{1}-{c}_{2}=-1 \\\\ 5{c}_{2}=10.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the second equation, we get ${c}_{2}=2.$ Substituting into the first equation, we obtain ${c}_{1}=1.$ \n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]{e}^{4t}+2[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]{e}^{-t}\\end{aligned}\n$$",
